\title[Frame definability over Markov chains]{Frame Definability over Markov Chains:\\
Soundness Spectra, Aperiodicity, and the Modal Content of Recurrence}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{22 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We develop a systematic correspondence between finite Markov chains and Kripke frames,
obtained by passing from a nonnegative transition matrix $P$ to its \emph{support frame},
whose accessibility relation is $P(u,v)>0$. The correspondence is exact on the level of
possibility: the $n$-step transition probability $P^n(u,v)$ is positive precisely when the
support frame carries a path of exactly $n$ edges from $u$ to $v$. Four groups of results
follow. First, row-stochasticity forces seriality, and seriality destroys converse
well-foundedness, so L\"ob's axiom $\Box(\Box\varphi\to\varphi)\to\Box\varphi$ is valid on no
nonempty Markov chain; the modal system of a chain is consistent, proves its own consistency
statement, is never L\"obian, and internalises its reflection schema exactly when the chain
is lazy. Second, the set of degrees $n$ for which $\Box^n\varphi\to\varphi$ holds at a state
coincides with the support of its return-time distribution and is an additive submonoid of
$\NN$; we prove that such a submonoid is cofinite if and only if no integer $d\ge 2$ divides
all of it, by a difference-subgroup argument avoiding generators, with explicit conductor
$y^2$ for consecutive elements $y,y+1$. Consequently, for a finite irreducible chain
primitivity is \emph{equivalent} to aperiodicity of a single state, with no self-loop and no
coprime-pair hypothesis. Third, a shortest-path principle proved by path excision yields
explicit primitivity exponents $N-1$ for a lazy chain on $N$ states and $2(N-1)$ from a
single holding state, the former sharp for the nearest-neighbour chain. Fourth, on a finite
frame the greatest fixed point of the diamond operator is exactly the set of worlds from
which a world lying on a cycle is reachable; the naive identification with the recurrent set
is refuted by a two-state absorbing chain. Finally, strong lumpability of Markov chains is
shown to \emph{be} a bounded morphism of support frames, so that validity transfer and modal
undefinability results apply verbatim to state aggregation.
\end{abstract}

